<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azemmour</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azemmour is a town on the Oum Er-Rbia river near its mouth on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Portugal took it in 1513 as one of its chain of fortified posts on the coast, but abandoned it in 1541 after the Saadi capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>. Its walled medina still overlooks the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azemmour.jpg" alt="Azemmour" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yaroslav Blanter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azemmour_from_Oum_Er-Rbia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Azemmour lies in El Jadida Province, on the left bank of the Oum Er-Rbia River, about 75 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> word azemmur, which means wild olive tree. The town is generally identified with the Punic Azama, latinised as Asama. Historically it served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribe of the Doukkala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1486 Azemmour was a dependency of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. In that year its inhabitants became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and tributaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">João II</a> of Portugal. In 1513 the governor, Moulay Zayam, refused to pay the tribute and gathered a large, well-equipped army. King Manuel of Portugal answered by sending a fleet of 500 ships carrying 15 thousand soldiers under Dom James, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a>.</p><p>He took the city on 1 September without resistance from its inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>, who later led the first circumnavigation of the earth, was among the Portuguese soldiers and lost his horse in skirmishes outside the city. Portuguese control was short. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">João III</a> of Portugal abandoned the city in 1541 because of the economic difficulties of his court.</p><p>Among the people associated with the town is Estevanico, also called Esteban the Moor. He was enslaved and travelled with a Spanish expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1527. He was one of four men out of several hundred who survived shipwrecks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> coasts, Native American slavery and attacks, and other setbacks over six years before reaching a Spanish colonial town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Azemmour has a medina in three parts: a Jewish quarter or mellah, a kasbah and the old medina. Local artists have decorated sections of the Old City walls. The patron saint of the town is Abu Shuayb, whose mausoleum was built on the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-iii-of-morocco">Mohammed ben Abdallah</a>, and a moussem is held each year in his honour. The Sidi Boubeker lighthouse stands 8 kilometres to the north, and the beach at Haouzia, with its eucalyptus and pine, is used for surfing and kitesurfing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azemmour” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sixteenth century Morocco was divided: the Wattasid sultans at Fez controlled only the north, local lords and religious brotherhoods held much of the rest, and the Portuguese had seized a chain of Atlantic ports including Safi, <b>Azemmour</b> and Agadir, then known as Santa Cruz do Cabo de Gué.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They dealt with the Portuguese posts at Safi and <b>Azemmour</b> and are described by Leo Africanus, who visited in the early 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hintata-emirate">Hintata emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad al-Ayyashi, a Sufi disciple appointed as governor near <b>Azemmour</b>, broke with the weak Saadi court and around 1614 began leading tribal holy war against the Christian forts, based at Salé.</blockquote><a href="/state/marabout-state-of-al-ayyashi">Marabout state of al-Ayyashi</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Manuel I the Portuguese built or seized Atlantic posts at Agadir, Safi, <b>Azemmour</b> and Mazagan between 1505 and 1514.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
